<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phraya Phahon Phonphayuhasena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Phot Phahonyothin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phraya Phahon Phonphayuhasena was a Thai army officer, the senior leader of the People's Party that carried out the revolution of 24 June 1932, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> in Siam. In June 1933 he overthrew the conservative government of Phraya Manopakorn in a coup and served as prime minister until 1938, when he was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaek-phibunsongkhram">Plaek Phibunsongkhram</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phraya-phahon-phonphayuhasena.jpg" alt="Phraya Phahon Phonphayuhasena" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phraya_Pahol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Phot Phahonyothin was born in Phra Nakhon Province, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, to a Teochew Thai Chinese father, Colonel Kim, and a Thai Mon mother, Chap. His family took the surname Phahonyothin in 1913 by royal decree. After attending the Chulachomklao Royal Military Academy, he was sent in 1903 on a royal scholarship to the Prussian Military Academy in Lichterfelde, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he was allegedly a classmate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>.</p><p>He then went to the Engineering College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, but completed only one year before his scholarship funds ran out, and he returned to Siam in 1912. In 1931 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajadhipok">Prajadhipok</a> raised him to the title Phraya Phahonphonphayuhasena with the rank of colonel, and in 1932 he became Commander of the Royal Siamese Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Phraya Phahon belonged to the conspirators known as the Four Musketeers, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khana-ratsadon">Khana Ratsadon</a>, or People's Party, which carried out the revolution of 1932. Afterwards three factions formed: the senior military faction under Phraya Phahon, a junior army and navy faction under Luang Phibunsongkhram, and a civilian faction under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pridi-banomyong">Pridi Banomyong</a>. As the most senior member he was seen as the de facto leader of the party, and he read the Declaration of the New Siamese State in the Royal Plaza, announcing the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>.</p><p>In March 1933 the Yellow Cover Dossier incident, which arose from Pridi's draft economic plan with socialist elements, led Prime Minister Phraya Manopakorn Nititada to expel Pridi from the cabinet and suspend the constitution. Phraya Phahon resigned on 15 June, citing health, and on 20 June led a bloodless coup. The next day he made himself the second prime minister of Siam, and the king reluctantly endorsed him. Phraya Manopakorn was exiled to British Malaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>His five years as prime minister were a struggle to keep power. The royalist Boworadet Rebellion broke out in October 1933, and government troops won after weeks of fighting. The cabinet was divided, and Luang Phibunsongkhram, as minister of defence, gained influence. A scandal over the sale of crown land to senior officials below market prices began in 1937, and the first direct elections to the People's Assembly took place on 7 November 1937. The cabinet resigned in September 1938 after budget problems, and elections in December made Phibunsongkhram prime minister. Phraya Phahon later served as Inspector General of the Royal Thai Armed Forces during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and died of a cerebral haemorrhage in February 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phraya Phahonphonphayuhasena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 24 June 1932 the People’s Party (Khana Ratsadon), a small group of civilian and military officials led by <b>Phraya Phahon Phonphayuhasena</b>, Pridi Banomyong and Plaek Phibunsongkhram, seized key points in Bangkok in a bloodless coup, detained leading princes and demanded a constitutional regime.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Phra Nakhon Province. Died in Paruskavan Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.77,100.51&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Paruskavan Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7304 1651 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Phraya Phahon Phonphayuhasena"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7754 1921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7754 1921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=13.77,100.51&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
